  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1122 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nCore of the coldest air continues to slowly shift east of the\nregion as high pressure builds into the western Great Lakes, \nallowing the lake effect cloud activity to diminish. Some early\nmorning ground fog will be possible on Sunday due to the calm \nwinds and clear skies. Expect high clouds to drift across the \nregion at times Sunday into early next week. Dry conditions will \nprevail into Wednesday as the high pressure slowly drifts to the \neast. A deep trough will move ashore on the west coast this \nweekend, then arrive in the Great Lakes Thursday, bringing the \nnext chance for rain to the area. Main surface low passes \nnear/just north of Lake Superior but a surface trough should bring\na period of gusty winds (up to ~35 mph) and some showers to the \nstate. It is quick moving and pretty moisture-starved, so only \nwill carry 20-40% rain chances overnight Wednesday into Thursday. \n\nThe next weather maker will be quick on the heels of the Thursday\none, likely arriving by Friday PM/evening. Initial push of WAA \nand warm front arrival will bring 20-50% shower chances, then a \nbetter signal for more widespread and heavier rain arrives \nSaturday into Sunday. Still some uncertainty on timing of the \ntime period for highest rain chances, but the highest chances \n(50-75%) remain centered around Saturday. Cold air will attempt to\nadvect into the northern side of the low pressure, but its arrival\nstill looks a little too late to bring any impactful snow to the\narea (<20% of 1\" of snow at LNL). But there are still a small\nminority of ensemble members that show a stronger system along\nwith a more southern track, which would result in some \naccumulating snow on the northern side of the system, so this \nthreat will need to be monitored.\n\nTemperatures...After a chilly start to the day on Sunday with most\nspots in the teens and 20s (a little warmer near Lake Michigan), \nand where some spots saw there coldest temps of the season, temps\nwill trend back well above normal early next week as return flow\nsets up. Highs in the mid 40s to low 50s are expected Sunday into\nMonday, then mainly 50s Tuesday into Wednesday, with some low 60s \npossible on Thursday. Some record warm minimums will be in \njeopardy mid-late next week (especially Thursday) with lows in \nthe 40s). Record highs look safe right now, as most are in the mid\nto upper 60s.",
